Rem ==================================================================
Rem json.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: JSON, RFC 8259
Rem
Rem A complete reader and writer, pure Shoddy. A document is a Json:
Rem
Rem     JNull | JBool | JNum | JStr | JArr | JObj
Rem
Rem An object's members are a List Of Pair — key in Fst, value in Snd —
Rem which is precisely dict.shoddy's association list, so DictGet,
Rem DictPut and friends work on them unchanged and JGet/JPut are thin
Rem wrappers rather than a second implementation.
Rem
Rem     Let doc = JsonParse(ReadFile("forecast.json"))
Rem     Let ps  = JPath(doc, { "properties", "periods" })
Rem     Print(JStrOr(JGet(JNth(ps, 1), "shortForecast"), "?"))
Rem     Print(JsonPretty(doc, 4))
Rem
Rem The guard-pair convention, the same shape as Val/ValOr and str's
Rem Bool/BoolOr: the strict word is the one you reach for first, and the
Rem total word is there the moment the input stops being yours.
Rem
Rem     JsonParse / JsonRead      JGet / JGetOr      JPath / JPathOr
Rem     JText / JStrOr            JNumber / JNumOr   JFlag / JBoolOr
Rem
Rem Shoddy has no catchable errors, so a parser that could only abort
Rem would be untestable on malformed input and unusable on untrusted
Rem input. JsonRead is therefore total: it returns JOk or JErr, and JErr
Rem carries the reason and the 1-based offset where the parse gave up.
Rem
Rem ---- known limits ------------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem NUL cannot be carried. Chr(0) is the empty string in Shoddy — "NUL
Rem ends a C string" — so a \u0000 escape is rejected as JErr rather
Rem than silently dropped, which would hand back a string shorter than
Rem the document said it was.
Rem
Rem Ten significant digits. Str prints an integral double under 1e15
Rem whole and everything else as C's %.10g, so writing 1 / 3 yields
Rem 0.3333333333 and a parse-write-parse round trip is not bit-exact
Rem for arbitrary doubles. That is the language's number formatting,
Rem not this machine's; values that survive ten digits round-trip
Rem exactly.
Rem
Rem Member order is document order — objects are lists, not sets. JPut
Rem has DictPut semantics (delete, then prepend), so putting a key that
Rem is already there MOVES it to the front. JsonCanonical sorts members
Rem by key, recursively, for anyone who needs a stable ordering.
Rem
Rem Duplicate keys: last wins. A repeated key is collapsed at parse
Rem time, keeping the last value at the first occurrence's position.
Rem
Rem Compare with JEqual, not with =. A List is a quotation and compares
Rem by identity, so two structurally identical JArr or JObj values are
Rem not = each other. JEqual is the deep comparison; it treats objects
Rem as unordered, as JSON does.
Rem
Rem Not JSON5. No comments, trailing commas, unquoted keys, single
Rem quotes, NaN/Infinity literals or hex numbers — all are errors.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "seq.shoddy"
Include "dict.shoddy"
Include "str.shoddy"

Type Json = JNull | JBool(JFlag As Boolean) | JNum(JNumber As Number) | JStr(JText As String) | JArr(JItems As List Of Json) | JObj(JMembers As List Of Pair)

Rem The public result of a total parse.
Type JRead = JOk(JDoc As Json) | JErr(JWhy As String, JAt As Number)

Rem The internal one, threaded through the whole recursive descent: every
Rem step returns its value AND the 1-based index of the next unconsumed
Rem character, or why and where it gave up.
Type JTake = JTook(JTVal As Json, JTNext As Number) | JTBad(JTWhy As String, JTAt As Number)

Rem ---- constants -------------------------------------------------------
Rem Machines cannot carry a top-level Let, so the constants are zero-arg
Rem Defs, as vt100's Esc() is. Shoddy source has no \b, \f or \uXXXX
Rem escape either — the lexer knows only \" \\ \n \t \r — so the quote
Rem and backslash characters are built with Chr.

Def JQt() As String
    Chr(34)

Def JBs() As String
    Chr(92)

Def JNl() As String
    Chr(10)

Rem Deeper than any real document, and it fails long before the CLR
Rem stack does. Value recursion is genuinely mutual and so is NOT
Rem compiled to a loop; [[[[[... is a two-byte-per-level stack bomb, and
Rem a .NET stack overflow cannot be caught. JsonReadDepth is the
Rem explicit escape hatch.
Def JsonMaxDepth() As Number
    200

Rem ======================================================================
Rem THE READER
Rem ======================================================================

Rem Whitespace is exactly these four characters. Not form feed, not
Rem vertical tab, not NBSP.
Def JIsWs(c As String) As Boolean
    c = " " Or c = Chr(9) Or c = JNl() Or c = Chr(13)

Def JSkipWs(s As String, i As Number) As Number
    If i > Len(s) Then
        i
    Else
        If JIsWs(Mid(s, i, 1)) Then
            JSkipWs(s, i + 1)
        Else
            i

Def JIsDigit(c As String) As Boolean
    c >= "0" And c <= "9"

Rem The index just past a run of digits. Self-tail-recursive, so it
Rem compiles to a loop and a long number costs no stack.
Def JDigits(s As String, i As Number) As Number
    If JIsDigit(Mid(s, i, 1)) Then
        JDigits(s, i + 1)
    Else
        i

Rem A hex digit's value, or -1. The stack-shaped word in the file: it
Rem takes one value and leaves one, which is exactly what the
Rem concatenative core is for. Ifte's branches are quotations, so only
Rem the chosen one runs.
Def JHexVal ( c -- n )
    Take c
    c 48 >= c 57 <= And [ c 48 - ] [ c 65 >= c 70 <= And [ c 55 - ] [ c 97 >= c 102 <= And [ c 87 - ] [ 0 1 - ] Ifte ] Ifte ] Ifte

Rem The value of the four hex digits at j, or -1 if they are not four
Rem hex digits (running off the end counts).
Def JHexQuad(s As String, j As Number) As Number
    If j + 3 > Len(s) Then
        0 - 1
    Else
        JHexQuadOf(JHexVal(Asc(Mid(s, j, 1))), JHexVal(Asc(Mid(s, j + 1, 1))), JHexVal(Asc(Mid(s, j + 2, 1))), JHexVal(Asc(Mid(s, j + 3, 1))))

Def JHexQuadOf(a As Number, b As Number, c As Number, d As Number) As Number
    If a < 0 Or b < 0 Or c < 0 Or d < 0 Then
        0 - 1
    Else
        a * 4096 + b * 256 + c * 16 + d

Rem The two-character escapes, as a lookup string.
Def JEscTwo() As String
    JQt() & JBs() & "/bfnrt"

Rem How many characters the escape at i occupies, or 0 if it is not a
Rem legal escape. i points AT the backslash.
Def JEscLen(s As String, i As Number) As Number
    Let c = Mid(s, i + 1, 1)
    If c = "u" Then
        If JHexQuad(s, i + 2) <= 0 Then
            0
        Else
            6
    Else
        If Len(c) = 1 And Instr(JEscTwo(), c) > 0 Then
            2
        Else
            0

Rem Why the escape at i is not legal. Only ever called when JEscLen said
Rem it was not.
Def JEscWhy(s As String, i As Number) As String
    Let c = Mid(s, i + 1, 1)
    If c = "u" Then
        If JHexQuad(s, i + 2) = 0 Then
            JBs() & "u0000 IS NOT REPRESENTABLE — A SHODDY STRING CANNOT CARRY NUL"
        Else
            "EXPECTED FOUR HEX DIGITS AFTER " & JBs() & "u"
    Else
        If Len(c) = 0 Then
            "UNTERMINATED STRING"
        Else
            "UNKNOWN ESCAPE " & JBs() & c

Rem The text an escape stands for. A \uXXXX becomes one UTF-16 code
Rem unit — Shoddy strings ARE .NET strings, so a surrogate pair arrives
Rem as its two units, is emitted as its two units, and concatenation
Rem gives back the astral character with no arithmetic. Combining the
Rem pair into a code point and calling Chr would silently truncate,
Rem since Chr casts to char. A lone surrogate is preserved as-is.
Def JEscText(s As String, i As Number) As String
    Let c = Mid(s, i + 1, 1)
    If c = "u" Then
        Chr(JHexQuad(s, i + 2))
    Else
        Select Case c
            Case "b"
                Chr(8)
            Case "f"
                Chr(12)
            Case "n"
                JNl()
            Case "r"
                Chr(13)
            Case "t"
                Chr(9)
            Case Else
                c

Rem A string, from its opening quote. The body scanner below tracks the
Rem start of the current unescaped RUN so an ordinary string — the
Rem overwhelming case — costs exactly one fragment, rather than one per
Rem character.
Def JStringAt(s As String, i As Number) As JTake
    JStrBody(s, i + 1, i + 1, { })

Def JStrBody(s As String, i As Number, run As Number, acc As List Of String) As JTake
    If i > Len(s) Then
        JTBad("UNTERMINATED STRING", i)
    Else
        Let c = Mid(s, i, 1)
        If c = JQt() Then
            JTook(JStr(Join(Reverse(JRun(s, run, i, acc)), "")), i + 1)
        Else
            If c = JBs() Then
                If JEscLen(s, i) = 0 Then
                    JTBad(JEscWhy(s, i), i)
                Else
                    JStrBody(s, i + JEscLen(s, i), i + JEscLen(s, i), Prepend(JEscText(s, i), JRun(s, run, i, acc)))
            Else
                If Asc(c) < 32 Then
                    JTBad("RAW CONTROL CHARACTER IN A STRING", i)
                Else
                    JStrBody(s, i + 1, run, acc)

Rem Flush the run [run, i) onto the fragment list, if it is not empty.
Def JRun(s As String, run As Number, i As Number, acc As List Of String) As List Of String
    If i > run Then
        Prepend(Mid(s, run, i - run), acc)
    Else
        acc

Rem ---- numbers ---------------------------------------------------------
Rem Val is strtod, which is markedly laxer than JSON — it takes a
Rem leading +, a bare .5, a trailing 5., and hex and infinity spellings.
Rem So the grammar
Rem
Rem     -? ( 0 | [1-9][0-9]* ) ( "." [0-9]+ )? ( [eE] [+-]? [0-9]+ )?
Rem
Rem is scanned and validated here, by hand, and Val is called only on a
Rem slice already known to match it.

Def JNumberAt(s As String, i As Number) As JTake
    Let a = Ifte(Mid(s, i, 1) = "-", i + 1, i)
    If Not JIsDigit(Mid(s, a, 1)) Then
        JTBad("EXPECTED A NUMBER", i)
    Else
        Rem A leading zero stands alone: 0 and 0.5 are numbers, 01 is not.
        Let b = Ifte(Mid(s, a, 1) = "0", a + 1, JDigits(s, a))
        If JIsDigit(Mid(s, b, 1)) Then
            JTBad("A LEADING ZERO CANNOT BE FOLLOWED BY A DIGIT", i)
        Else
            JNumFrac(s, i, b)

Def JNumFrac(s As String, i As Number, b As Number) As JTake
    If Mid(s, b, 1) <> "." Then
        JNumExp(s, i, b)
    Else
        Let c = JDigits(s, b + 1)
        If c = b + 1 Then
            JTBad("EXPECTED DIGITS AFTER THE DECIMAL POINT", c)
        Else
            JNumExp(s, i, c)

Def JNumExp(s As String, i As Number, c As Number) As JTake
    Let e = Mid(s, c, 1)
    If e <> "e" And e <> "E" Then
        JTook(JNum(Val(Mid(s, i, c - i))), c)
    Else
        Let sign = Mid(s, c + 1, 1)
        Let f = Ifte(sign = "+" Or sign = "-", c + 2, c + 1)
        Let g = JDigits(s, f)
        If g = f Then
            JTBad("EXPECTED DIGITS IN THE EXPONENT", f)
        Else
            JTook(JNum(Val(Mid(s, i, g - i))), g)

Rem ---- values, arrays, objects -----------------------------------------
Rem These three are genuinely mutually recursive and so consume stack in
Rem proportion to NESTING DEPTH, which is small for real documents and
Rem unbounded on adversarial input — hence the depth budget d, spent one
Rem level at a time. The item and member loops, by contrast, call only
Rem themselves in tail position, so a long array costs no stack at all.

Def JValue(s As String, i As Number, d As Number) As JTake
    Let k = JSkipWs(s, i)
    If k > Len(s) Then
        JTBad("UNEXPECTED END OF INPUT", k)
    Else
        Select Case Mid(s, k, 1)
            Case JQt()
                JStringAt(s, k)
            Case "{"
                JObjectAt(s, k, d)
            Case "["
                JArrayAt(s, k, d)
            Case "t", "f", "n"
                JLiteralAt(s, k)
            Case Else
                JNumberAt(s, k)

Def JLiteralAt(s As String, i As Number) As JTake
    If Mid(s, i, 4) = "true" Then
        JTook(JBool(True), i + 4)
    Else
        If Mid(s, i, 5) = "false" Then
            JTook(JBool(False), i + 5)
        Else
            If Mid(s, i, 4) = "null" Then
                JTook(JNull(), i + 4)
            Else
                JTBad("EXPECTED true, false OR null", i)

Def JArrayAt(s As String, i As Number, d As Number) As JTake
    If d <= 0 Then
        JTBad("NESTING TOO DEEP", i)
    Else
        Let k = JSkipWs(s, i + 1)
        If Mid(s, k, 1) = "]" Then
            JTook(JArr({ }), k + 1)
        Else
            JArrItems(s, k, d, { })

Def JArrItems(s As String, i As Number, d As Number, acc As List Of Json) As JTake
    Select Case JValue(s, i, d - 1)
        Case JTBad(why, at)
            JTBad(why, at)
        Case JTook(v, n)
            Let k = JSkipWs(s, n)
            Select Case Mid(s, k, 1)
                Case ","
                    JArrItems(s, k + 1, d, Prepend(v, acc))
                Case "]"
                    JTook(JArr(Reverse(Prepend(v, acc))), k + 1)
                Case Else
                    JTBad("EXPECTED , OR ] IN AN ARRAY", k)
        Case Else
            JTBad("EXPECTED A VALUE IN AN ARRAY", i)

Def JObjectAt(s As String, i As Number, d As Number) As JTake
    If d <= 0 Then
        JTBad("NESTING TOO DEEP", i)
    Else
        Let k = JSkipWs(s, i + 1)
        If Mid(s, k, 1) = "}" Then
            JTook(JObj({ }), k + 1)
        Else
            JObjMembers(s, k, d, { })

Rem One Def, not two, so the member loop is a SELF tail call and a wide
Rem object costs no stack. The nested pattern Case JTook(JStr(key), n)
Rem destructures the parsed key and the position it ended in one clause.
Def JObjMembers(s As String, i As Number, d As Number, acc As List Of Pair) As JTake
    Let k = JSkipWs(s, i)
    If Mid(s, k, 1) <> JQt() Then
        JTBad("EXPECTED A QUOTED KEY IN AN OBJECT", k)
    Else
        Select Case JStringAt(s, k)
            Case JTBad(why, at)
                JTBad(why, at)
            Case JTook(JStr(key), n)
                Let c = JSkipWs(s, n)
                If Mid(s, c, 1) <> ":" Then
                    JTBad("EXPECTED : AFTER AN OBJECT KEY", c)
                Else
                    Select Case JValue(s, c + 1, d - 1)
                        Case JTBad(why2, at2)
                            JTBad(why2, at2)
                        Case JTook(v, n2)
                            Let e = JSkipWs(s, n2)
                            Select Case Mid(s, e, 1)
                                Case ","
                                    JObjMembers(s, e + 1, d, Prepend(Pair(key, v), acc))
                                Case "}"
                                    JTook(JObj(JDedupe(Reverse(Prepend(Pair(key, v), acc)))), e + 1)
                                Case Else
                                    JTBad("EXPECTED , OR } IN AN OBJECT", e)
                        Case Else
                            JTBad("EXPECTED A VALUE AFTER AN OBJECT KEY", c)
            Case Else
                JTBad("EXPECTED A QUOTED KEY IN AN OBJECT", k)

Rem Last duplicate wins, and the survivor keeps the FIRST occurrence's
Rem position — the same rule JavaScript and Python apply. Walk the
Rem members forwards, emitting each key once; the value comes from the
Rem reversed list, where the last occurrence is the one DictGet reaches
Rem first. A linear scan per key, as dict.shoddy is throughout: objects
Rem run to tens of members, not thousands.
Def JDedupe(ms As List Of Pair) As List Of Pair
    JDedupeR(ms, Reverse(ms), { })

Def JDedupeR(ms As List Of Pair, back As List Of Pair, acc As List Of Pair) As List Of Pair
    If IsEmpty(ms) Then
        Reverse(acc)
    Else
        If DictHas(acc, Fst(First(ms))) Then
            JDedupeR(Rest(ms), back, acc)
        Else
            JDedupeR(Rest(ms), back, Prepend(Pair(Fst(First(ms)), DictGet(back, Fst(First(ms)))), acc))

Rem ---- the public reader -----------------------------------------------

Def JsonReadDepth(s As String, maxDepth As Number) As JRead
    Select Case JValue(s, 1, maxDepth)
        Case JTBad(why, at)
            JErr(why, at)
        Case JTook(v, n)
            Let k = JSkipWs(s, n)
            If k > Len(s) Then
                JOk(v)
            Else
                JErr("TRAILING CHARACTERS AFTER THE DOCUMENT", k)
        Case Else
            JErr("UNEXPECTED END OF INPUT", 1)

Def JsonRead(s As String) As JRead
    JsonReadDepth(s, JsonMaxDepth())

Def JsonParse(s As String) As Json
    Select Case JsonRead(s)
        Case JOk(v)
            v
        Case JErr(why, at)
            Error("JSONPARSE: " & why & " AT " & Str(at))
        Case Else
            Error("JSONPARSE: UNREACHABLE")

Def JsonLoad(path As String) As Json
    JsonParse(ReadFile(path))

Rem ======================================================================
Rem THE WRITER
Rem ======================================================================
Rem Never build a string with & in a left fold — that is quadratic in the
Rem output, which is exactly the shape of a serializer. Every writer word
Rem prepends FRAGMENTS onto an accumulator (so the list is built
Rem backwards, in constant time per fragment) and the join happens once,
Rem at the top, in JsonText and JsonPretty.

Def JsonText(j As Json) As String
    Join(Reverse(JFrags(j, { })), "")

Def JFrags(j As Json, acc As List Of String) As List Of String
    Select Case j
        Case JNull
            Prepend("null", acc)
        Case JBool(b)
            Prepend(Ifte(b, "true", "false"), acc)
        Case JNum(n) Where n <> n
            Error("JSONTEXT: nan IS NOT JSON")
        Case JNum(n) Where Not IsFinite(n)
            Error("JSONTEXT: " & Str(n) & " IS NOT JSON")
        Case JNum(n)
            Prepend(Str(n), acc)
        Case JStr(t)
            Prepend(JQt(), Prepend(JsonEscape(t), Prepend(JQt(), acc)))
        Case JArr(xs)
            Prepend("]", JFragItems(xs, Prepend("[", acc), True))
        Case JObj(ms)
            Prepend("}", JFragMembers(ms, Prepend("{", acc), True))

Rem Two traps live in these four lines, and both are silent.
Rem
Rem Note the If rather than Ifte on the separator. Ifte CALLS the branch
Rem it chooses, and a List is a quotation — so Ifte(lead, acc, ...) would
Rem RUN the accumulator instead of returning it, scattering its fragments
Rem over the stack. Ifte is for scalars; a branch that yields a list or a
Rem quotation needs If.
Rem
Rem And the flag is named lead, not first: words are case-folded and a
Rem local outranks a builtin, so a parameter named first would shadow
Rem First everywhere in the Def — First(xs) would quietly read the flag.
Def JFragItems(xs As List Of Json, acc As List Of String, lead As Boolean) As List Of String
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        acc
    Else
        If lead Then
            JFragItems(Rest(xs), JFrags(First(xs), acc), False)
        Else
            JFragItems(Rest(xs), JFrags(First(xs), Prepend(",", acc)), False)

Def JFragMembers(ms As List Of Pair, acc As List Of String, lead As Boolean) As List Of String
    If IsEmpty(ms) Then
        acc
    Else
        If lead Then
            JFragMembers(Rest(ms), JFrags(Snd(First(ms)), JFragKey(Fst(First(ms)), acc, "")), False)
        Else
            JFragMembers(Rest(ms), JFrags(Snd(First(ms)), JFragKey(Fst(First(ms)), Prepend(",", acc), "")), False)

Rem A key, its quotes, and the separator that follows it.
Def JFragKey(k As String, acc As List Of String, gap As String) As List Of String
    Prepend(":" & gap, Prepend(JQt(), Prepend(JsonEscape(k), Prepend(JQt(), acc))))

Rem There is no IsFinite or IsNaN builtin, and Abs(x) > 1e308 does not
Rem work — the largest finite double is about 1.797e308, so that test
Rem rejects perfectly good numbers. Subtraction is exact: a finite value
Rem gives 0, either infinity gives inf - inf = nan, and nan gives nan.
Def IsFinite(x As Number) As Boolean
    x - x = 0

Rem ---- escaping --------------------------------------------------------
Rem " and \ always; the short forms for the five that have them; every
Rem other code unit below 32 as \u00XX in lowercase hex. NOT / — legal
Rem but noisy, and it breaks byte-comparison against every other
Rem producer. NOT non-ASCII — that goes out raw.

Def JsonEscape(s As String) As String
    Join(Reverse(JEscFrags(s, 1, 1, { })), "")

Def JEscFrags(s As String, i As Number, run As Number, acc As List Of String) As List Of String
    If i > Len(s) Then
        JRun(s, run, i, acc)
    Else
        If JNeedsEsc(Mid(s, i, 1)) Then
            JEscFrags(s, i + 1, i + 1, Prepend(JEscOne(Mid(s, i, 1)), JRun(s, run, i, acc)))
        Else
            JEscFrags(s, i + 1, run, acc)

Def JNeedsEsc(c As String) As Boolean
    Asc(c) = 34 Or Asc(c) = 92 Or Asc(c) < 32

Def JEscOne(c As String) As String
    Select Case Asc(c)
        Case 34
            JBs() & JQt()
        Case 92
            JBs() & JBs()
        Case 8
            JBs() & "b"
        Case 12
            JBs() & "f"
        Case 10
            JBs() & "n"
        Case 13
            JBs() & "r"
        Case 9
            JBs() & "t"
        Case Else
            JBs() & "u00" & JHex2(Asc(c))

Def JHex2(n As Number) As String
    JHexDigit(Fix(n / 16)) & JHexDigit(n Mod 16)

Def JHexDigit(n As Number) As String
    Mid("0123456789abcdef", n + 1, 1)

Rem ---- pretty ----------------------------------------------------------
Rem The python -m json.tool / JSON.stringify(x, null, n) convention, so
Rem the output diffs against other tools: one member per line, ": "
Rem between key and value, a comma at the end of every line but the
Rem last, the closing bracket at the parent's indent. An empty array or
Rem object stays on one line.

Def JsonPretty(j As Json, indent As Number) As String
    If indent <= 0 Then
        JsonText(j)
    Else
        Join(Reverse(JPFrags(j, indent, 0, { })), "")

Def JPFrags(j As Json, indent As Number, level As Number, acc As List Of String) As List Of String
    Select Case j
        Case JArr(xs)
            If IsEmpty(xs) Then
                Prepend("[]", acc)
            Else
                Prepend("]", Prepend(JPad(indent, level), Prepend(JNl(), JPItems(xs, indent, level + 1, Prepend("[", acc), True))))
        Case JObj(ms)
            If IsEmpty(ms) Then
                Prepend("{}", acc)
            Else
                Prepend("}", Prepend(JPad(indent, level), Prepend(JNl(), JPMembers(ms, indent, level + 1, Prepend("{", acc), True))))
        Case Else
            JFrags(j, acc)

Def JPItems(xs As List Of Json, indent As Number, level As Number, acc As List Of String, lead As Boolean) As List Of String
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        acc
    Else
        JPItems(Rest(xs), indent, level, JPFrags(First(xs), indent, level, JPLine(indent, level, acc, lead)), False)

Def JPMembers(ms As List Of Pair, indent As Number, level As Number, acc As List Of String, lead As Boolean) As List Of String
    If IsEmpty(ms) Then
        acc
    Else
        JPMembers(Rest(ms), indent, level, JPFrags(Snd(First(ms)), indent, level, JFragKey(Fst(First(ms)), JPLine(indent, level, acc, lead), " ")), False)

Rem The line break before an item: a comma first unless it is the first.
Def JPLine(indent As Number, level As Number, acc As List Of String, lead As Boolean) As List Of String
    If lead Then
        Prepend(JPad(indent, level), Prepend(JNl(), acc))
    Else
        Prepend(JPad(indent, level), Prepend(JNl(), Prepend(",", acc)))

Def JPad(indent As Number, level As Number) As String
    StrRep(" ", indent * level)

Rem Compact on disk. Use WriteFile(path, JsonPretty(j, 2)) for a file
Rem meant to be read by a person or diffed in a review.
Def JsonSave(path As String, j As Json)
    WriteFile(path, JsonText(j))

Rem ---- canonical -------------------------------------------------------
Rem Sort is documented as ascending over all-numbers or all-strings, so
Rem it will not sort a List Of Pair by key. Sort the KEYS — a List Of
Rem String — and map them back through the member list to rebuild.

Def JsonCanonical(j As Json) As Json
    Select Case j
        Case JObj(ms)
            JObj(Map(Sort(JKeys(j)), Fn(k) => Pair(k, JsonCanonical(DictGet(ms, k)))))
        Case JArr(xs)
            JArr(Map(xs, JsonCanonical))
        Case Else
            j

Rem ======================================================================
Rem NAVIGATION AND CONSTRUCTION
Rem ======================================================================
Rem JGet, JHas, JPut, JDel and JKeys delegate to dict.shoddy rather than
Rem reimplementing it: an object's members ARE an association list.
Rem The raw accessors JText, JNumber, JFlag, JItems and JMembers come
Rem free from the Type and abort on the wrong variant — they are the
Rem strict half of each guard pair, so they are not wrapped.

Def JHas(j As Json, k As String) As Boolean
    Select Case j
        Case JObj(ms)
            DictHas(ms, k)
        Case Else
            False

Def JGet(j As Json, k As String) As Json
    If JHas(j, k) Then
        DictGet(JMembers(j), k)
    Else
        Error("JGET: NO KEY '" & k & "'")

Def JGetOr(j As Json, k As String, dflt As Json) As Json
    If JHas(j, k) Then
        DictGet(JMembers(j), k)
    Else
        dflt

Rem 1-based, like every other index in the language. Not Nth — that is a
Rem builtin, and a Def of a builtin's name is refused.
Def JNth(j As Json, n As Number) As Json
    Nth(JItems(j), n)

Def JPath(j As Json, path As List Of String) As Json
    If IsEmpty(path) Then
        j
    Else
        JPath(JGet(j, First(path)), Rest(path))

Def JPathOr(j As Json, path As List Of String, dflt As Json) As Json
    If IsEmpty(path) Then
        j
    Else
        If JHas(j, First(path)) Then
            JPathOr(JGet(j, First(path)), Rest(path), dflt)
        Else
            dflt

Rem Document order. Point-free: the members, mapped through Fst.
Def JKeys(j As Json) As List Of String
    JMembers(j)
        Map(Fst)

Def JCount(j As Json) As Number
    Select Case j
        Case JObj(ms)
            Length(ms)
        Case JArr(xs)
            Length(xs)
        Case Else
            Error("JCOUNT: NOT AN OBJECT OR AN ARRAY")

Def IsJNull(j As Json) As Boolean
    j = JNull()

Rem ---- total coercions -------------------------------------------------

Def JStrOr(j As Json, dflt As String) As String
    Select Case j
        Case JStr(t)
            t
        Case Else
            dflt

Def JNumOr(j As Json, dflt As Number) As Number
    Select Case j
        Case JNum(n)
            n
        Case Else
            dflt

Def JBoolOr(j As Json, dflt As Boolean) As Boolean
    Select Case j
        Case JBool(b)
            b
        Case Else
            dflt

Rem ---- functional update -----------------------------------------------

Def JPut(j As Json, k As String, v As Json) As Json
    JObj(DictPut(JMembers(j), k, v))

Def JDel(j As Json, k As String) As Json
    JObj(DictDel(JMembers(j), k))

Def JNumList(xs As List Of Number) As Json
    JArr(Map(xs, JNum))

Def JStrList(xs As List Of String) As Json
    JArr(Map(xs, JStr))

Rem ---- deep equality ---------------------------------------------------
Rem A List is a quotation and compares by identity, so = is only
Rem structural down to the first JArr or JObj. This is the comparison
Rem you want for a round trip. Objects are treated as unordered, as JSON
Rem says they are; arrays are ordered, as JSON says they are.

Def JEqual(a As Json, b As Json) As Boolean
    Select Case Pair(a, b)
        Case Pair(JArr(xs), JArr(ys))
            Length(xs) = Length(ys) And All(Zip(xs, ys), Fn(p) => JEqual(Fst(p), Snd(p)))
        Case Pair(JObj(ms), JObj(ns))
            Length(ms) = Length(ns) And All(ms, Fn(p) => DictHas(ns, Fst(p)) And JEqual(Snd(p), DictGet(ns, Fst(p))))
        Case Else
            a = b
